# Training and evaluation

{% if problem_type == 'forecasting' %}
This example uses chronological 60/20/20 origin cutoffs shared across series. Rows whose
labels cross the next origin cutoff are purged; `gap_hours` can make the purge stricter.
The model predicts a direct three-hour horizon with one- and 24-hour lags and known
future hour-of-day encodings. It requires regular hourly data and fails gaps or duplicate
series timestamps. Forecasting never uses a random split.
{% elif problem_type == 'classification' %}
This independent-observation classification example uses seeded, stratified 60/20/20
partitions. Both split stages preserve class proportions. Choose a grouped or temporal
strategy when repeated entities or time-dependent outcomes introduce leakage risks.
{% else %}
This independent-observation regression example uses seeded random 60/20/20 partitions.
Choose a grouped or temporal strategy when repeated entities or time-dependent outcomes
introduce leakage risks.
{% endif %}

`--tune` compares depths on validation only. The default fits one depth. After selection,
refit on train plus validation and evaluate test once. Neither model selection nor
baseline computation uses test data. Tests verify disjoint partitions, chronological
label boundaries and invariance of past features to changed future values.

Remote MLflow runs record parameters, final test metrics, signatures, input examples,
input dataset digest, source reference, revision, environment versions and the baseline.
Models register in `databricks-uc` with three-part names. Training sets `candidate` only.
QA and PROD inference require a numeric model version supplied at deployment.

## Promotion

For a shared metastore, an authorized release operator can call MLflow's
`MlflowClient.copy_model_version` with `models:/<source catalog>.<schema>.model/<version>`
and the destination model name. Evaluate the copied artifact on representative QA data,
record both source and destination versions, and deploy the exact approved PROD version.
Do not retrain during deployment or resolve a moving alias in QA/PROD. With separate
metastores, export and verify the immutable artifact, signature and dependencies, then
register it at the destination. This release process needs organization-specific access
and is deliberately not an automatic cross-workspace credential flow.

The registered model also carries `extra_files/baseline.json` and `provenance.json`.
These travel with an approved artifact copy, so monitoring does not need credentials
for the source workspace training run.
